== a brother or sister calls the night the work went
tier: everyday
stages: young_adult adult mature
age: 20-70
calls: BR
rate: 1
tone: trouble
life: family, work
horizon: months
roles: sibling, parent, partner
who: sibling
cast_want: far_hard
touch: work
earth: a brother or sister in another town calls late at night: something there has cost them their job, and they do not know what comes next
times: Most adults see a brother or sister lose a job at some point; when a big employer in a town shuts, many families there have someone out of work at once (estimate)
gap: 3-8
scene: The phone rings late. It is {sibling}, calling from {their_town}, voice flat: {far_event}, and with it went the job, the wages and the plan for the year. They are not asking for anything yet. They just needed to tell someone who knows them.
scene W: When one of the family falls, the rest stand up. {N} owes {sibling} more than a few kind words on the phone.
scene U: {N} is already thinking it through: what that trade pays elsewhere, what support there is, how long the savings might last.
scene B: {N} can see where this goes: a sibling out of work becomes a sibling who needs things, and {Ns} own job is not so safe either.
scene R: The flatness in the voice is worse than tears. {N} wants to be on the next bus, right now.
scene G: People in this family have lost work before and come through. Towns change; the family is what stays.
- W | promise to call every evening until they are back on their feet | habit | v: benevolence, conformity | mark: kept your word | chance: 72
- U | sit up with them and list every option, from retraining to moving | v: self-direction, benevolence | chance: 82
- B | listen kindly, but make clear you cannot carry them for long | v: security, power | act: listen kindly, but make clear they cannot carry the sibling for long | chance: 76
- R | take the first bus there in the morning, just to be with them | v: benevolence, stimulation | chance: 86
- G | tell them the family has come through worse, and will again | v: tradition, benevolence | chance: 66
- W>U | look up their rights: the pay owed, the notice, the support to claim | door | v: universalism, security | chance: 68
- U>B | ask around at your own work for an opening that would suit them | door | v: achievement, benevolence | act: ask around at their own work for an opening that would suit the sibling | chance: 58
- B>R | send them a month's wages at once, so they can breathe | v: benevolence | mark: helped someone in need | tags: pay:money-.03 | chance: 64
- R>G | get the whole family on a video call that same night | v: benevolence, tradition | tech: video calls | chance: 54
- G>W | tell them to stay put and stand with the others who lost work there | v: tradition, universalism | chance: 74
works: {sibling} hangs up steadier, and calls again two nights later with the start of a plan.
works: The worst of the shock passes, and {sibling} says later that the call that night is what got them through.
fails: {sibling} goes quiet for weeks, and {N} hears the news only through the family.
fails: The job hunt in {their_town} drags on, and every call sounds a little more tired.

