touch: home
earth: a parent in another town calls: since the trouble there their home is gone or no longer fit to live in, and they have nowhere settled to go
times: Losing a home to a flood, a fire or a sudden rent rise is something many families meet once in a lifetime, often through an older relative; about 1 household in 50 has to leave its home in a given year (estimate)
gap: 3-8
scene: Late in the evening {parent} calls from {their_town}, voice steady in the way it gets when things are bad: since {far_event}, the old home cannot be lived in, and half the street is scrambling too. {parent} is sleeping on a friend's sofa for now and says it is fine, really. Behind the words {N} can hear how small the room is.
scene W: A parent who kept a roof over {Ns} head for twenty years should not be sleeping on someone else's sofa. Some things are simply owed.
scene U: {N} wants to know what is true before anything is decided: the insurance, the landlord, the council, and how long it could all take.
